theory.intro:
Prisms and pyramids are both named after their base, but they are built differently. A prism has a **uniform cross-section**; a pyramid comes to a point.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (P1) at (0,1);
\coordinate (P2) at (-0.951,0.309);
\coordinate (P3) at (-0.588,-0.809);
\coordinate (P4) at (0.588,-0.809);
\coordinate (P5) at (0.951,0.309);
\coordinate (Q1) at (1.992,2.15);
\coordinate (Q2) at (1.041,1.459);
\coordinate (Q3) at (1.404,0.341);
\coordinate (Q4) at (2.580,0.341);
\coordinate (Q5) at (2.943,1.459);
\fill[gray!30,opacity=0.45] (P1)--(P2)--(P3)--(P4)--(P5)--cycle;
\fill[gray!30,opacity=0.45] (Q1)--(Q2)--(Q3)--(Q4)--(Q5)--cycle;
\draw (P1)--(P2)--(P3)--(P4)--(P5)--cycle;
\draw (Q4)--(Q5)--(Q1);
\draw[dashed] (Q1)--(Q2)--(Q3)--(Q4);
\draw (P1)--(Q1);
\draw (P4)--(Q4);
\draw (P5)--(Q5);
\draw[dashed] (P2)--(Q2);
\draw[dashed] (P3)--(Q3);
\node[font=\small] at (0,0) {base};
\node[font=\small] at (1.992,1.15) {base};
\node[font=\small] at (1.0,-2.1) {Prism};
\coordinate (B1) at (7,-0.15);
\coordinate (B2) at (6.049,-0.461);
\coordinate (B3) at (6.412,-0.964);
\coordinate (B4) at (7.588,-0.964);
\coordinate (B5) at (7.951,-0.461);
\coordinate (T) at (7,1.9);
\fill[gray!30,opacity=0.45] (B1)--(B2)--(B3)--(B4)--(B5)--cycle;
\draw (B2)--(B3)--(B4)--(B5);
\draw[dashed] (B5)--(B1)--(B2);
\draw (T)--(B2);
\draw (T)--(B3);
\draw (T)--(B4);
\draw (T)--(B5);
\draw[dashed] (T)--(B1);
\node[font=\small,above] at (T) {apex};
\node[font=\small] at (7,-0.6) {base};
\node[font=\small] at (7,-2.1) {Pyramid};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

